Hordeolum (stye)
Common Infections and Afflictions
Painful localized tender lump, Eyelid margin erythema, Visible yellow pustule

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-017
Reason for Visit: Acute severe eye pain and rapidly enlarging, localized eyelid swelling.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acutely painful, rapidly enlarging erythematous mass on the eyelid margin causing significant distress, a severe foreign body sensation, and reactive tearing that impairs functional vision. Given the proximity to the orbit and the rapid onset, an immediate on-site clinical assessment is required to definitively rule out preseptal or acute orbital cellulitis, which are sight-threatening emergencies. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of allowing a deeper orbital infection to progress, strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours MBS item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: There is a massive, agonizing lump on my eyelid, and it feels exactly like a shard of glass is trapped in my eye.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 24 hours ago with mild localized tenderness on the right upper eyelid margin. This rapidly evolved over the last 12 hours into an acutely painful, localized tender lump. The patient reports severe focal erythema and edema of the affected eyelid and a constant, sharp sensation of a foreign body in the eye. Mild reactive tearing is present secondary to the mechanical irritation. Patient reports intact visual acuity. Acknowledges a history of chronic meibomian gland dysfunction.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently keeping the affected eye closed due to pain upon blinking.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to facial pain and fear of ocular damage.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/82 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Alert. Right eye is held voluntarily shut. No systemic distress or rigors.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A bacterial culture of purulent discharge confirming Staphylococcus aureus would assist in confirming the diagnosis in refractory cases, but is not acutely indicated.
Physical Exam: Right eye examination reveals a distinct, acutely tender, focal erythematous and edematous nodule on the upper eyelid margin. A visible yellow pustule is present precisely at the base of an eyelash follicle. The underlying bulbar and palpebral conjunctiva are entirely normal and uninflamed, ruling out diffuse conjunctivitis. Extraocular movements are completely full and painless. Visual acuity is grossly intact. No proptosis. Pupillary reflexes are normal.
Environmental Observations: Standard residential environment. Safe access to warm water in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has aggressively rubbed the eye, exacerbating the inflammation.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Hordeolum - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Preseptal Cellulitis - Ruled out definitively as the erythema and edema are strictly focal to the eyelid margin rather than diffusely involving the entire eyelid and periorbital tissue; no systemic fever.
Chalazion - Unlikely as the primary acute diagnosis given the extreme focal tenderness and the presence of an active pustule at the lid margin, whereas a chalazion is typically a painless, chronic, deeper granulomatous nodule (though an acute hordeolum can evolve into a chalazion).
Dacryocystitis - Ruled out due to the lesion's location on the upper lid margin rather than over the medial canthal lacrimal sac.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of a rapidly swelling, highly painful periorbital mass mandated an urgent home evaluation to definitively exclude sight-threatening conditions such as preseptal or orbital cellulitis. Direct physical examination confirmed a localized, benign staphylococcal abscess of the eyelash follicle (hordeolum). By performing a thorough ocular exam and ruling out deeper orbital involvement, an unnecessary emergency department presentation was successfully averted. Initiating targeted conservative management and strict hygiene protocols required immediate intervention that could not safely wait until standard consulting hours, fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Advised regular Paracetamol 1000mg up to four times daily for analgesia. Prescribed Chloramphenicol 1% eye ointment to be applied to the lid margin twice daily for 5 days strictly to prevent secondary conjunctival infection upon spontaneous rupture of the pustule.
Positive Behavioral Change: To accelerate the natural drainage of the abscess, apply a clean, remarkably warm (but not scalding) wet compress directly to the closed eyelid for 10 minutes, exactly 4 times a day. The sustained heat directly melts the thickened, infected oils trapping the bacteria, physically allowing the pustule to come to a head and drain without dangerous surgical intervention.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3 days if the swelling increases diffusely or if the pain becomes unmanageable.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness spreads to the rest of your eyelid or cheek, if you experience any double vision, or if the eye itself becomes red and painful to move.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical. The lesion is a localized bacterial micro-abscess.
Clinical Course: The pustule will typically point and spontaneously rupture within 48 to 72 hours, leading to immediate pain relief. The residual localized swelling may take 1 to 2 weeks to completely resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a fever, or if the vision in the affected eye becomes blurry or restricted.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: You have a small, infected blocked oil gland right at the base of an eyelash. The goal is to use heat to melt the blockage so it drains naturally.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely do not attempt to squeeze, pop, or lance the lump yourself. The venous drainage of the face connects directly to the brain; squeezing the abscess can physically push the staphylococcal bacteria backward into your central circulation, causing a lethal infection. Wash your hands rigorously before and after touching your face.

